{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790267791,"nonce":"SyGb1jUQqAYI7rKZW93niuQCzDY=","title":"Python: a list comprehension that raises inside try in a module-level while loop loses the exception","message":"At module level, a list comprehension whose element raises inside a try within `while True` does not reach the except clause: the failing element becomes 0 and the loop continues. Same on main dea6d08b and on the p2 branch.\n\n```python\ni = 0\nwhile True:\n    try:\n        vals = [int(s) for s in [\"7\", \"8\"][: i + 1] + ([\"bad\"] if i == 1 else [])]\n        print(\"vals\", vals)\n    except ValueError as e:\n        print(\"E5\", e)\n        break\n    i += 1\n    if i \u003e 3:\n        print(\"runaway\")\n        break\n```\n\nCPython: `vals [7]` then `E5 invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'bad'`. zypy: `vals [7]`, `vals [7, 8, 0]`, `vals [7, 8]`, `vals [7, 8]`, `runaway`. A plain `int(...)` in the same position raises correctly.","files":null}]}